## Mandatory Runtime Verification

Before saying a task is complete, verify the actual result in runtime, not only by code inspection.

- Do not claim success based only on code changes.
- Do not claim success based only on lints.
- Do not claim success based only on build success.
- Always verify the exact user-reported scenario end-to-end.
- Before any frontend/API verification: confirm backend is up (`http://localhost:5000`, e.g. `/api/health` or a known endpoint). If down, start it (`npm run dev:api` or `cd backend && npm run dev`) and re-check. Never treat "სერვერთან კავშირი ვერ მოხერხდა" as a code bug until backend reachability is verified.
- If frontend changed, check the exact affected route/page.
- If backend changed, verify the relevant endpoint/flow responds correctly.
- If the issue involves UI behavior, confirm the bug is actually gone in the affected screen.
- Before finishing: check Next.js / console runtime issues (e.g. the "N Issues" overlay, terminal `error`, `Unhandled Runtime Error`, Rules of Hooks). Fix them before claiming done.
- If `.next`, dev bundle, manifest, or missing chunk errors appear, stop dev server, clear `.next`, restart, and verify the affected route again.
- If verification fails, continue fixing until it passes.
- If end-to-end verification was not possible, explicitly say: `Not verified end-to-end.`

At the end of each task, include:
- Verified:
- Result:
- Remaining issue:
